<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sahib Ata Fakhr al-Din Ali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1288">1288</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Sahib Ata</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sahib Ata Fakhr al-Din Ali was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the Seljuk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> for several decades under Mongol overlordship. A great builder, he founded mosques, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravanserai">caravanserais</a>, among them the Sahib Ata complex and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gok-madrasa">Gök Medrese</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>. In the 1270s his sons were granted Karahisar and nearby lands, founding the Sahib Ataid principality.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sahib-ata-fakhr-al-din-ali.jpg" alt="Sahib Ata Fakhr al-Din Ali" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Intension at English Wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thinminaret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
